1
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,764
For thousands of years, we have dreamed of
minds made by human hands.

2
00:00:31,314 --> 00:00:33,461
Bronze giants in Greek myth.

3
00:00:33,661 --> 00:00:36,860
Clockwork figures that played music and
wrote letters.

4
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:45,039
And in 1843, Ada Lovelace imagined an
engine that could weave algebra, the way a loom weaves flowers.

5
00:00:47,389 --> 00:00:56,200
In 1943, Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts
showed that simple switches, wired like neurons, could compute logic.

6
00:00:56,650 --> 00:00:58,825
The artificial neuron was born.

7
00:01:01,375 --> 00:01:04,790
In 1950, Alan Turing proposed a test.

8
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:10,786
If a machine could talk so well that we
couldn't tell it from a person, could we still say it doesn't think?

9
00:01:13,336 --> 00:01:19,121
In the summer of 1956, a workshop at
Dartmouth College gave the dream its name.

10
00:01:20,171 --> 00:01:21,952
Artificial intelligence.

11
00:01:23,002 --> 00:01:30,041
Its founders believed every aspect of
learning could be described so precisely that a machine could simulate it.

12
00:01:30,491 --> 00:01:34,011
They thought real progress could be made
in a single summer.

13
00:01:36,861 --> 00:01:44,500
Two years later, Frank Rosenblatt unveiled
the Perceptron, a machine that learned to recognize patterns on its own.

14
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:50,815
Newspapers predicted it would one day
walk, talk, and be conscious of its own existence.

15
00:01:53,165 --> 00:01:58,705
In 1966, a program called ELIZA imitated a
psychotherapist.

16
00:01:59,255 --> 00:02:00,810
It understood nothing.

17
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:06,780
Yet its creator's own secretary asked him
to leave the room, so she could talk to it in private.

18
00:02:09,730 --> 00:02:12,158
But the promises outran the machines.

19
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:17,036
Computers were too slow, the world too
complex, and the funding froze.

20
00:02:17,986 --> 00:02:20,176
The first AI winter had begun.

21
00:02:21,526 --> 00:02:25,140
In the 1980s, expert systems brought a
brief thaw.

22
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:27,628
Then, a second winter set in.

23
00:02:31,378 --> 00:02:35,805
Through the long cold, a few researchers
kept faith in neural networks.

24
00:02:36,255 --> 00:02:43,469
In 1986, they popularized backpropagation,
a way for networks to learn from their own mistakes.

25
00:02:43,919 --> 00:02:48,540
Soon, Yann LeCun's networks were reading
handwritten digits on envelopes.

26
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:57,364
In 1997, IBM's Deep Blue defeated the
world chess champion, Garry Kasparov.

27
00:02:57,814 --> 00:03:01,530
It could examine two hundred million
positions every second.

28
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:03,841
But chess was all it could do.

29
00:03:06,291 --> 00:03:08,335
Then, three forces collided.

30
00:03:08,785 --> 00:03:10,989
Oceans of data from the internet.

31
00:03:11,189 --> 00:03:13,570
Graphics chips built for video games.

32
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:16,025
And networks deeper than ever before.

33
00:03:16,575 --> 00:03:24,155
In 2012, a network called AlexNet crushed
the ImageNet challenge, created by Fei-Fei Li and her team.

34
00:03:24,805 --> 00:03:27,190
The deep learning revolution had begun.

35
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:38,436
In 2016, DeepMind's AlphaGo challenged Lee
Sedol, one of the greatest Go players of all time.

36
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:43,193
Go has more possible positions than there
are atoms in the universe.

37
00:03:43,843 --> 00:03:49,156
In game two, on move thirty-seven, AlphaGo
played a move no human would have played.

38
00:03:50,706 --> 00:03:52,957
Commentators thought it was a mistake.

39
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:54,442
It was brilliant.

40
00:03:54,892 --> 00:03:57,677
AlphaGo won the match, four games to one.

41
00:04:00,427 --> 00:04:07,733
In 2017, eight researchers at Google
published a paper with a bold title: Attention Is All You Need.

42
00:04:08,282 --> 00:04:12,889
Their Transformer could weigh every word
against every other word, all at once.

43
00:04:13,139 --> 00:04:14,310
And it scaled.

44
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:20,914
Language models grew from millions of
parameters to billions.

45
00:04:21,164 --> 00:04:24,037
They learned to write, to translate, and
to code.

46
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:31,532
Then, on November 30th, 2022, ChatGPT put
that power into everyone's hands.

47
00:04:32,582 --> 00:04:35,890
One hundred million people tried it within
two months.

48
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,977
Since then, the pace has only quickened.

49
00:04:41,327 --> 00:04:44,435
AI now writes and paints, speaks and sees.

50
00:04:44,885 --> 00:04:48,470
It has predicted the shapes of nearly
every known protein.

51
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:54,473
In 2024, its pioneers received Nobel
Prizes in physics and chemistry.

52
00:04:54,923 --> 00:05:00,824
It solves olympiad mathematics, writes
software, and more and more, it acts on our behalf.

53
00:05:04,974 --> 00:05:09,418
More than seventy years ago, Alan Turing
asked whether machines could think.

54
00:05:10,068 --> 00:05:12,074
We are still debating the answer.

55
00:05:12,924 --> 00:05:15,873
But a new question now belongs to all of
us.
